! Contact Card Overview

This tiddler demonstrates the two contact card templates for `text/vnd.tiddlywiki-multiple+fields` tiddlers tagged ''ContactCard''.

---

!! Compact List View

Uses `$:/core/ui/contact-card-list-item` — designed for overviews and lists.

<div class="tc-contact-list-grid">
<$list filter="[tag[ContactCard]sort[title]]">
<$transclude tiddler="$:/core/ui/contact-card-list-item"/>
</$list>
</div>

---

!! Full Card View

Each tiddler's view template renders the full card automatically (click a name above to navigate). Below is an inline transclude of each contact's full card:

<$list filter="[tag[ContactCard]sort[title]]">

<div style="margin-bottom:1.5em;">

!!! <$link/>

<$transclude tiddler="$:/core/ui/ViewTemplate/body/contact-card"/>

</div>

</$list>

---

!! Filter Examples

;All contacts
:`[tag[ContactCard]]` → <$text text={{{ [tag[ContactCard]count[]] }}}/> contacts

;Business contacts
:`[tag[ContactCard]each:compound-field[category]field:category[business]]` — filter by compound field values

;Favorites
:<$list filter="[tag[ContactCard]] :filter[get[favorite]match[yes]]"><$link/> </$list>

;All email addresses across contacts
:<$list filter="[tag[ContactCard]]" variable="contact"><$list filter="[<contact>compound-field-names[]] :filter[<contact>compound-field-type<currentTiddler>match[email]]" variable="fn"><$text text={{{ [<contact>get<fn>] }}}/>, </$list></$list>

---

!! Presentation headers

[[ContactTemplate]] carries the labels, tooltips and placeholders, plus two rules: `birthday` has `hide-filter: [{!!category}match[business]]` and `role` has `readonly-filter: [{!!category}match[personal]]`. Press Edit on a card and switch the category to watch the birthday appear and the role lock. See [[CompoundFieldHeaders]].

!! Strict or loose

Each contact follows the template in one of two ways, see [[CompoundSchema]]. Jane, Alice and Nina use `inherit-schema`: the template owns their entries, its headers are copied into their text, and the edit template locks their types. Mario and Bob use `inherit-compound`: their own headers win, so Mario's website is labelled ''Homepage'' and Bob's company ''Startup'' and his birthday ''Born'', and their types stay editable.

<table>
<tr><th>Contact</th><th>Field</th><th>Schema</th><th>Own headers</th></tr>
<$list filter="[tag[ContactCard]sort[title]]">
<tr><td><$link/></td><td><$text text={{{ [<currentTiddler>has[inherit-schema]then[inherit-schema]else[inherit-compound]] }}}/></td><td><$text text={{{ [<currentTiddler>compound-schema[]] }}}/></td><td><$text text={{{ [<currentTiddler>compound-schema:strict[]then[ignored, rewritten on save]else[win over the schema]] }}}/></td></tr>
</$list>
</table>

!! Your own form

The `compound-field` procedure builds a form anywhere. This table edits [[SampleContact-Nina]] in place, listing `compound-field-names:visible[]`:

<$let currentTiddler="SampleContact-Nina">
<table>
<$list filter="[<currentTiddler>compound-field-names:visible[]!match[body]]" variable="fieldName">
<tr><th><$transclude $variable="compound-field-label" fieldName=<<fieldName>>/></th><td><$transclude $variable="compound-field" fieldName=<<fieldName>> mode="edit"/></td></tr>
</$list>
</table>
</$let>

The same procedure works on a standard tiddler when the type is given in the call, see [[SampleContact-Plain]].
